<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Appian Way</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">312 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Via Appia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Appian Way (Via Appia) was a Roman road begun in 312 BCE by the censor Appius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> Caecus, first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Capua and later extended to Brundisium on the Adriatic. Built to move armies during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a>, it became one of the main arteries of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Long stretches with their original paving and roadside tombs survive near Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/appian-way.jpg" alt="Appian Way" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LuisaV72 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paesaggio_dell%27Appia_antica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
